  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence from pmid-26244699 explicitly states that the A391E substitution over-stabilizes FGFR3 dimers, leading to increased activity of the mutant receptor. This mechanistic description directly supports the claim that the FGFR3 A391E variant is a gain-of-function mutation. The evidence further links this enhanced dimerization propensity to the pathology in Crouzon syndrome with acanthosis nigricans, confirming the functional significance of the variant.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-26244699",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 430,
        "quote": "The difference in dimer stability due to the A391E substitution was measured as -1.4 kcal/mole. Thus, the A391E mutation over-stabilizes the FGFR3 dimers, suggesting that the increased activity of the mutant receptor is due to its enhanced dimerization propensity. . . . his structural change, along with the increased dimerization propensity, is likely contributing to the pathology in Crouzon syndrome with acanthosis nigricans."
      }
    ],
